<title>ARGENTINA: Argentine weekly official crop progress.</title>
<headline>Argentine weekly official crop progress.</headline>
<dateline>BUENOS AIRES 1997-02-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Argentine farmers had sown 99.1 percent of their 1996/97 maize crop by January 31, the Agriculture Secretariat said in its weekly crop report Thursday.</p>
<p>There have been serious outbreaks of Rio Cuarto disease in maize in northwestern Buenos Aires, northern La Pampa and southern Santa Fe, and less serious outbreaks in eastern Cordoba and northern Buenos Aires. But it is hard to say how much damage to output will be caused by the insect-born virus -- which stunts plant growth. Soybean plants were progressing well, although rains came late in some parts of Entre Rios.  </p>
<p>Intended total planting (thousands of hectares) and the percentage already sown (year-earlier in brackets). Harvest levels (thousands of hectares) and as a percentage of planted area, (year-earlier in brackets). Yield in quintals per hectare.</p>
<p> ..........Planting.............Harvest..................Yield</p>
<p> Wheat     6,983  100.0(100.0) 6,694.4  100.0   (n/a)    (n/a)</p>
<p> Maize     3,782   99.1 (99.8)     n/a    n/a   (n/a)    (n/a)</p>
<p> Soybeans  6,412   99.6 (99.3)     n/a    n/a   (n/a)    (n/a)</p>
<p> Sunflower 3,039  100.0(100.0)     3.1    n/a   (n/a)    (n/a)</p>
<p> Sorghum     777   98.7 (98.2)     n/a    n/a   (n/a)    (n/a)</p>
<p> Cotton	964   99.8  (n/a)     n/a    n/a   (n/a)    (n/a)</p>
<p>((--Jason Webb, Buenos Aires Newsroom +541-318-0655))</p>
